# Line endings, decided once instead of per commit.
#
# This file exists because of a near miss. The repository was built by uploading
# files through the GitHub web UI, which stored them with CRLF, while the working
# copy on disk had LF for some files and CRLF for others. Committing the LF copies
# would have produced a whole-file diff on top of the real change — 8,600 changed
# lines to hide 350 — and the fix that time was converting two files by hand
# before staging them. That works exactly once and then someone forgets.
#
# `text=auto` puts the decision in git's hands: every file git considers text is
# normalised to LF in the object store, whatever the working copy happens to use.
# Diffs then show what changed rather than which editor last saved the file.

* text=auto

# Shell scripts must be LF in the worktree as well as in the repository. bash
# treats a trailing \r as part of the command and fails with messages that point
# nowhere near the cause, and these two are run under WSL.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries, listed explicitly. Without this, `text=auto` guesses from content, and
# a PNG that happens to look textual enough would be "normalised" — which for an
# image means corrupted, silently, in a way that only shows up when someone looks
# at the installer icon.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.zip binary

# The updater signing key is gitignored and must never be committed, but if one
# ever reaches the index by accident it should at least not be mangled on the way.
*.key binary
*.pub binary
